  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/.... \nThe subtropical high will remain entrenched across the region \nthrough at least the end of the week, with 500 mb height fields \nremaining steady between 595-597dam. Therefore, temperatures will \ncontinue to run a good 4-7 degrees above normal as afternoon highs\nacross the lower deserts will range between 108-113 degrees. \nThere will also be little overnight relief as an increase in low- \nlevel moisture will cause overnight lows to only bottom out \nbetween the mid 80s to low 90s with the low 90s expected in the \nPhoenix area. Therefore, widespread Major HeatRisk will continue \nwith Extreme Heat Warnings currently in effect through Friday \nevening. Extension of this warning may be warranted through next\nweekend as a weakening and slight re-positioning of the high\nfurther away may not come until Sunday-Monday next week.\n\nThe latest guidance does show the high eventually moving \nnortheastward into the Four Corners region by the end of the week \ninto next weekend, putting the region under a more favorable \nmoisture advection pattern as the east to southeast flow \nincreases. Therefore, an increase in monsoonal activity is more \nlikely during this timeframe, mostly across the higher terrain \nareas. Although, it will certainly be possible for convective \nactivity to impact the south-central AZ lower deserts at some \npoint as well, it is still too far out to know what days will be\nmost active as it will be dependent on mesoscale processes such \nas outflow evolutions and/or any easterly waves, which are \ndifficult for models to resolve several days out. As of right \nnow, NBM PoPs across the south-central AZ lower deserts starting \nnext Thursday range between 10-30%.",
